<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<object type="traktor.mcp.SkillAsset">
	<name>custom-uikit-widget</name>
	<description>Author a bespoke UiKit widget whose art is an SVG imported as a Spark movie, and preview it with a Scaffolding test page.</description>
	<whenToUse><![CDATA[Asked for on-screen UI that the stock UiKit widgets cannot express — a health/stamina bar with real art, an animated icon, a gauge, a custom meter — or asked to give a widget bespoke art, or to make a live preview/test page for a widget. For HUD labels, buttons and modal menus from stock widgets, use uikit-hud-menus instead.]]></whenToUse>
	<body><![CDATA[# Custom UiKit widget from SVG art

Build a `Widget` subclass whose graphics are authored as an **SVG imported as a Spark `MovieAsset`**, then animate the named sprites inside it. Use this when `Static`/`PushButton`/`ProgressBar`/`Image` cannot express the element. For stock HUD labels, buttons and modal menus see *uikit-hud-menus*; for script idioms see *traktor-lua-scripting*.

## 1. Author the SVG

A `MovieAsset` whose `fileName` ends in `.svg` is imported by `ConvertSvg`. It turns every element carrying a **`traktor:sprite`** attribute into a sprite character, **named by its `inkscape:label`, falling back to its `id`**:

- **top level** → exported under that name: this is what `Widget._createResource(parent, "{{resource}}")` instantiates;
- **nested** → placed on its parent's frame under that name: this is what `mc:getMember("fill")` returns, i.e. the parts you animate.

Inkscape gives layers and renamed groups an `inkscape:label`, and that label silently replaces the id as the name. Names may repeat between sprites (ids must be unique), so use labels for repeated part names.

```xml
<svg width="460" height="56" viewBox="0 0 460 56"
     xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
     xmlns:traktor="http://traktor/svg">
  <g id="{{resource}}" traktor:sprite="1" style="display:inline">
    <g id="plate" traktor:sprite="1">
      <polygon points="66,12 446,12 454,20" style="fill:#1e1e24"/>
    </g>
    <g id="fill" traktor:sprite="1">
      <rect x="62" y="16" width="388" height="24" style="fill:#ffffff"/>
    </g>
  </g>
</svg>
```

Rules the importer imposes on the authoring:

- **Every `traktor:sprite` / `traktor:frame` group needs a name.** An unnamed one ABORTS the import at that point: it, everything after it in the document, and the pending shapes of enclosing sprites are dropped, yet the movie still builds with no error. A path with no segments, or a `<text>` with no name or an unknown font, does the same.
- **Put every drawable inside a named child sprite.** Shapes owned directly by a sprite are placed on its frame *after* its child sprites, so they would cover them. With one sprite per layer, depth order is document order.
- `viewBox="0 0 W H"` with matching `width`/`height` gives 1 SVG unit = 1 pixel of widget space.
- **Author each exported widget at the origin.** Coordinates end up absolute in movie space, so a widget authored at x=470 would draw 470px off. Several widgets in one file therefore overlap; toggle `display:none` per layer to edit them, exactly as `data/Assets/System/UiKit/Resources/UiKit2.svg` does.
- **Style is not inherited from `<g>`**: every drawable needs its own `style="fill:#rrggbb;fill-opacity:0.45"`. A `fill=` *attribute* makes the parser ignore `style` entirely, so use one or the other. Colours must be 6-digit hex; gradients (`fill:url(#g)`) are not imported and come out black.
- Geometry: `rect` (with `rx`/`ry`), `circle`, `ellipse`, `polygon`, `polyline`, `path` (`M L H V C S Q T Z`), `image`, `text`.
  - `A` (arc) degenerates to a straight line, and `T` is approximate.
  - `image` must be an embedded base64 PNG (`xlink:href`).
  - Path winding doesn't matter, and holes work: a loop enclosed by an odd number of other loops is a hole.
- Transforms: the arguments are split on commas only. Write `translate(10,20)` and `scale(s,s)`, or `matrix(...)`: `translate(10 20)` reads as (10, 0), and a one-argument `scale(s)` zeroes y.
- Per-state art: a `<g traktor:frame="1">` inside a sprite becomes a keyframe named by its label, for `mc:gotoAndStop("on")`. Keep the shared art outside the frame groups.
- Resizable art: `traktor:scalingGrid="x y width height"` on a sprite sets the centre cell of a 9-grid; the corners keep their size when the sprite is scaled.
- `<text>` becomes an `Edit` field only if it has a name, a `font-family` in its `style` that is listed in the MovieAsset's `fonts`, a `shape-inside:url(#rect)` pointing at a `<rect>` in `<defs>` (its size is the text bounds), and its text inside a `<tspan>`. It is always placed at the sprite origin. Parenting a UiKit `Static` to the widget is usually simpler and matches the rest of the UI.

## 2. Import and register it

- Create a `traktor.spark.MovieAsset`, `fileName` relative to `Pipeline.AssetPath` (e.g. `UI/Hud.svg`).
- Add its guid to `Widget.initialize(resourceManager, soundPlayer, { ... })` in **every stage that uses the widget**. Keep UiKit2 `\{B45977D0-3D89-4FA7-BE4C-FAA9ED63A942}` in the list (`Static`, `Dialog`, … come from there). `Widget._createResource` takes the first kit that has the name.

## 3. The widget class

```lua
{{name}} = {{name}} or class("{{name}}", Widget)

{{name}}:setStyle("resource", "{{resource}}")

local W < const > = 460   -- must match the SVG
local H < const > = 56

function {{name}}:new(parent, skin)
	local mc < const > = Widget._createResource(parent, skin or self:getStyle().resource)
	assert(mc ~= nil, "{{name}}; no such movie resource, is the movie in the resource kits?")
	self:_initialize(parent, mc)
end

function {{name}}:getPreferredSize()
	return { width = W, height = H }
end

function {{name}}:_initialize(parent, mc)
	Widget._initialize(self, parent, mc)

	-- Fixed size art: never let a layout stretch the placement past it.
	self._halign = Widget.ALIGN_CENTER
	self._valign = Widget.ALIGN_CENTER

	self._fill_mc = mc:getMember("fill")
	assert(self._fill_mc ~= nil, "{{name}}; movie resource is missing its parts")
end
```

A plain `Widget` lays out **no** children, so a `Static` parented to it must be placed by hand in `place()`, and again whenever its text changes, or it keeps the position it was centred at.

## 4. Animate it

Drive transforms and colour only; never rebuild geometry at runtime.

- `mc.X` / `mc.Y` (pixels), `mc.xScale` / `mc.yScale` / `mc.scale` (**percent**, `Vector2`), `mc.visible`, `mc.alpha` (0..1), `mc.colorTransform = traktor.spark.ColorTransform(mul, add)`.
- **Tint trick**: author the art **white** (or greyscale for shading) and recolour it per frame with a colour transform. The transform multiplies, so white gives the exact target colour; one call restyles a whole shaded bar (e.g. green → amber → red by health).
- Expose an `:update(dt)` and call it from the `Stage` each frame. Calling it above the paused/game-over early-returns lets animations settle while the game is frozen.

**PIVOT GOTCHA.** Because the importer bakes shapes at absolute movie coordinates, a child sprite's origin is the *movie's* origin, not its art's, so scaling one pushes it away from that origin. Translate back to pin a pivot:

```lua
-- Horizontal scale about the vertical line x = px (bar fills).
local function scaleX(mc, px, s)
	mc.xScale = s * 100
	mc.X = px * (1 - s)
end

-- Uniform scale about (px, py) (icons that pulse in place).
local function scaleAbout(mc, px, py, s)
	mc.scale = Vector2(s * 100, s * 100)
	mc.X = px * (1 - s)
	mc.Y = py * (1 - s)
end
```

To make a part disappear, set `mc.visible = false` rather than scaling it to 0.

## 5. Preview it with a Scaffolding page

`traktor.uikit.Scaffolding` gives a live editor preview that rebuilds whenever a script or the art is saved. It is the fastest loop for widget work, and it needs no game running.

- Create a `traktor.uikit.Scaffolding` instance whose `scaffoldingClass` is the guid of a script defining a `Scaffolding` subclass (`#using \{218D113A-66DC-9049-8424-701FF60F2BC4}`).

```lua
{{name}}Scaffolding = {{name}}Scaffolding or class("{{name}}Scaffolding", Scaffolding)

function {{name}}Scaffolding:getResourceKits()
	return { "\{<your movie guid>}", "\{B45977D0-3D89-4FA7-BE4C-FAA9ED63A942}" }
end

function {{name}}Scaffolding:setup(frame)
	self._widget = {{name}}(Panel(frame, TableLayout({ 0 }, { 0 }, 24, 24, 0, 14)))
end

function {{name}}Scaffolding:update()
	Scaffolding.update(self)
	self._widget:update(1 / 60)   -- plus whatever drives the widget's state
end
```

- The editor's preview control constructs the class with `(database, resourceManager, root, width, height)` and calls `update()` every tick, so scripted state changes belong there. Show a few instances at fixed values next to one driven through its whole state loop, so every animation path is exercised.
- Examples to copy: `System/Examples/UiKit`.

## Gotchas (hard-won)

- **`--` is illegal inside an XML comment.** A malformed SVG fails the import with `XML parse error at line N`, and has been seen to take the *editor* down, so validate the file before building it.
- In path `d`, `points` and `viewBox` the number scanner **skips a leading `.`**, so `.5` reads as `5`. Always emit a leading digit (`0.5`).
- **Check what was imported.** A silently truncated import (see the naming rule) is the usual cause of missing parts. The editor log (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\Traktor\Editor\Logs\Editor_*.log`) prints one `Enter sprite …` line per sprite.
- `build_asset {rebuild:true}` skips conversion when the SVG content is unchanged and already built. Touch the file (even a comment) to force it through.
- To keep a bad SVG away from the editor, build the movie headlessly: `Traktor.Pipeline.App -s=<cfg> -n -v -f "{movie guid}"`.
  - Run it from a sandbox folder holding `<cfg>.config`. Its settings point at a `binary=false` output database and list `Traktor.Database.Local` and `Traktor.Spark.Editor` in `Editor.Modules`.
  - On Linux prefix `LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<bin>`; on Windows run the exe from `bin\latest\win64\releaseshared`.
  - The converted movie is then XML you can read: check `exports`, the character tree (nested sprite names) and the fill colours.
- `Container:getPreferredSize` **memoizes** `_cachedEstimate`, which is cleared only when a child is added or removed. A widget whose preferred size follows its content therefore never triggers a re-layout. Prefer a fixed-size widget and re-place its own text, or clear the caches up the parent chain and call `getAncestor():update()` yourself.
- The dynamic canvas API (`SpriteInstance:createCanvas`, `traktor.spark.Canvas`) is exposed to script and looks like an easy way to draw widget art, but it has not been exercised in a long time. Prefer imported movie art.
- Generating the SVG from a small script keeps chamfers, hexagons and curves exact and re-tunable; the result is still a normal SVG that can be edited in Inkscape afterwards.]]></body>
	<engineVersion/>
	<published>false</published>
	<parameters>
		<item type="traktor.mcp.SkillParameter">
			<name>name</name>
			<description>Widget class name</description>
			<defaultValue>HealthBar</defaultValue>
		</item>
		<item type="traktor.mcp.SkillParameter">
			<name>resource</name>
			<description>Exported sprite name in the SVG</description>
			<defaultValue>MC_HealthBar</defaultValue>
		</item>
	</parameters>
</object>
